THREE FUNDAMENTAL SERIES
============================================================

  ALL reciprocals: 1 + 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/4 + ... = -log(1-x)
    exp -> 1/(1-x)

  ODD reciprocals: 1 + 1/3 + 1/5 + 1/7 + ... = arctanh(x)
    exp(2*.) -> (1+x)/(1-x) = Q(x) [TOURNAMENT]

  EVEN reciprocals: 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/6 + ... = -(1/2)log(1-x^2)
    exp(2*.) -> 1/(1-x^2) [GRAPH]

  KEY IDENTITY: -log(1-x) = arctanh(x) - (1/2)log(1-x^2)
  => 1/(1-x) = sqrt(Q(x)) * sqrt(1/(1-x^2))
  => 1/(1-x) = sqrt(Q(x)/(1-x^2)) = sqrt((1+x)/(1-x)^3)... hmm

  Actually: exp(-log(1-x)) = 1/(1-x)
  exp(arctanh(x)) = sqrt((1+x)/(1-x))
  exp(-(1/2)log(1-x^2)) = 1/sqrt(1-x^2)
  Product: sqrt((1+x)/(1-x)) / sqrt(1-x^2)
  = sqrt((1+x)/(1-x)) / sqrt((1-x)(1+x))
  = 1/(1-x). CHECK!

============================================================
m-TH POWERS: TOURNAMENTS vs MULTISETS
============================================================

Tournament: Q^m  = ((1+x)/(1-x))^m = 1 + 2*sum g_k(m)*x^k
Multiset:   S^m  = (1/(1-x))^m = sum C(m+k-1,k)*x^k

Q^m counts DIRECTED weighted matchings (Delannoy)
S^m counts UNDIRECTED multisets (stars-and-bars)

m=3:
  k=1: Q^m=     6, S^m=     3, Q/S=2
  k=2: Q^m=    18, S^m=     6, Q/S=3
  k=3: Q^m=    38, S^m=    10, Q/S=19/5
  k=4: Q^m=    66, S^m=    15, Q/S=22/5
  k=5: Q^m=   102, S^m=    21, Q/S=34/7
  k=6: Q^m=   146, S^m=    28, Q/S=73/14
  k=7: Q^m=   198, S^m=    36, Q/S=11/2

m=5:
  k=1: Q^m=    10, S^m=     5, Q/S=2
  k=2: Q^m=    50, S^m=    15, Q/S=10/3
  k=3: Q^m=   170, S^m=    35, Q/S=34/7
  k=4: Q^m=   450, S^m=    70, Q/S=45/7
  k=5: Q^m=  1002, S^m=   126, Q/S=167/21
  k=6: Q^m=  1970, S^m=   210, Q/S=197/21
  k=7: Q^m=  3530, S^m=   330, Q/S=353/33

The ratio Q^m/S^m = the DIRECTED FRACTION of all orderings.
As k grows: this ratio -> 2^k * k! / m^k (approximately)
The directed content GROWS with interaction order k.

============================================================
DECOMPOSITION: NATURAL = DIRECTED + UNDIRECTED
============================================================

(1/(1-x))^m = Q(x)^{m/2} * (1/(1-x^2))^{m/2}

In the tournament context:
  FULL structure (1/(1-x)^m):
    = ALL interactions at all scales
    = directed + undirected together

  DIRECTED part (Q^{m/2}):
    = interactions sensitive to ARC DIRECTION
    = generated by ODD harmonics 1, 1/3, 1/5, ...
    = the tournament-specific contribution

  UNDIRECTED part (1/(1-x^2))^{m/2}:
    = interactions INSENSITIVE to direction
    = generated by EVEN harmonics 1/2, 1/4, 1/6, ...
    = the graph-like (symmetric) contribution

============================================================
WHAT IS A NATURAL NUMBER?
============================================================

In our framework, 1/n is the weight of the n-th interaction scale:

  n= 1: weight 1/1, ODD (directed, tournament)
         meaning: pairwise comparison itself
  n= 2: weight 1/2, EVEN (undirected, graph)
         meaning: whether a unit step occurred (either direction)
  n= 3: weight 1/3, ODD (directed, tournament)
         meaning: 3-way directed consistency (triangle orientation)
  n= 4: weight 1/4, EVEN (undirected, graph)
         meaning: 2 unit steps co-occurring (undirected pair)
  n= 5: weight 1/5, ODD (directed, tournament)
         meaning: 5-way directed cluster (pentagon interaction)
  n= 6: weight 1/6, EVEN (undirected, graph)
         meaning: 3 unit steps or 2 triples (mixed undirected)
  n= 7: weight 1/7, ODD (directed, tournament)
         meaning: 7-way directed cluster
  n= 8: weight 1/8, EVEN (undirected, graph)
         meaning: 4 unit steps or 2 quads
  n= 9: weight 1/9, ODD (directed, tournament)
  n=10: weight 1/10, EVEN (undirected, graph)
  n=11: weight 1/11, ODD (directed, tournament)
  n=12: weight 1/12, EVEN (undirected, graph)

The NATURAL NUMBER n is the SCALE of the n-th interaction.
Its reciprocal 1/n is the STRENGTH of that interaction.

The harmonic series sum 1/n diverges: CRITICALITY.
All scales contribute; no scale is negligible.

The tournament selects only the ODD scales (1, 3, 5, 7, ...)
because arc direction breaks the even symmetry.

============================================================
THE CAYLEY HIERARCHY
============================================================

Each level of the natural numbers corresponds to an operator:

  n=1: Q^{1/2} = sqrt((1+x)/(1-x))
       The HALF-Cayley: one square root of direction

  n=2: Q^1 = (1+x)/(1-x)
       The FULL Cayley: tournament weights (our main result)

  n=3: Q^{3/2}
       The SESQUI-Cayley: 3-fold directed interaction

More generally: Q^{m/2} for integer m gives the m-th
level of the directed hierarchy.

And the NATURAL NUMBER m indexes these levels:
  m=1: Q^{1/2}, the square root of direction
  m=2: Q, the basic Cayley (binary tournament)
  m=3: Q^{3/2}
  ...
  m -> infinity: Q^{m/2} -> (wild growth)

============================================================
TOURNAMENT-GRAPH DUALITY
============================================================

TOURNAMENT (directed): Q = exp(2*arctanh)
  Odd spectrum: 1, 1/3, 1/5, 1/7, ...
  Functional equation: Q(x)*Q(-x) = 1
  Even g_k determined by odd g_k

GRAPH (undirected): G = exp(-log(1-x^2)) = 1/(1-x^2)
  Even spectrum: 1/2, 1/4, 1/6, 1/8, ...
  Functional equation: G(x) = G(-x) (even function)
  Odd coefficients are zero

NATURAL NUMBERS (full): N = Q^{1/2} * G^{1/2}
  Full spectrum: 1, 1/2, 1/3, 1/4, ...
  No functional equation constraint
  All coefficients independent

So: TOURNAMENTS are the ODD PART of the natural numbers.
    GRAPHS are the EVEN PART.
    The natural numbers are the PRODUCT of both.

The DIFFERENCE between tournaments and graphs IS the
difference between odd and even, between direction and symmetry,
between arctanh and log, between Q and 1/(1-x).

And this difference is precisely the NATURAL NUMBERS THEMSELVES:
each n tells you at what scale the directed and undirected
contributions split apart.

n=1: directed (ODD)
n=2: undirected (EVEN)
n=3: directed (ODD)
n=4: undirected (EVEN)
...

The natural numbers ARE the alternation between
tournament and graph, direction and symmetry,
odd and even, antisymmetric and symmetric.

In the beginning was the COMPARISON: A > B or B > A?
This single binary question, iterated and composed,
generates all of mathematics through the Cayley transform.
The natural numbers enumerate the SCALES at which the
question echoes. And the echo alternates: directed,
undirected, directed, undirected, forever.
